  and no shared validation module, no other forms, and no test or lint tooling to hang it on. So this isn't a bounded
  edit to an existing flow; it's introducing a validation layer plus a plan for how forms consume it. I'll work the full
  brainstorming process: questions, approaches, a sectioned design, then a written spec — no code until you approve.

  What I found in the repo:
  - [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — the whole webapp: [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, [38;5;153mlogin()[39m, [38;5;153mvalidateForm()[39m (presence-check only, hardcoded to
    [38;5;153musername[39m/[38;5;153mpassword[39m), and a submit listener that reads DOM elements by literal id.
  - [38;5;153mindex.html[39m — one login form, loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as a plain [38;5;153m<script>[39m (no modules, no bundler).
  - [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m / [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m — a CommonJS [38;5;153mgreet[39m demo, entirely disconnected from the webapp. Two module systems
    coexist in this repo today.
  - [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m — no dependencies, no scripts, no test runner.

  That split matters: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is browser-global script, [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS. Any shared validation module has to pick a home
  and a module format, and that choice is hard to walk back once a second form depends on it.

